message.content:
Based on my systematic examination (full overview plus progressive zooms of the maxillary and mandibular apical regions, high-pass/contrast enhancement, and difference-of-Gaussians blob detection), the radiograph shows a full dentition with several restorations and mild–moderate horizontal bone loss. Most interdental dark zones proved to be crestal/periodontal spaces rather than apical lesions. The two focal, reasonably circumscribed periapical/furcation radiolucencies I could verify are reported below.

{"lesions": [{"box": [0.607, 0.437, 0.643, 0.488], "pai": 3}, {"box": [0.641, 0.495, 0.677, 0.553], "pai": 3}]}

Note: the first box is a small rounded radiolucency at the furcation/periapex of the maxillary molar on the image-right side; the second is a similar small defined radiolucency at the furcation/periapex of the mandibular molar on the image-right side. Both show bone-structure change with some mineral loss without the strongly corticated, larger radiolucency that would justify a 4 or 5. Panoramic images superimpose structures and distort the apical region, so these would need intraoral periapicals to confirm and grade definitively.
